-- guide-listening-traps-premium: 24 sections, 4,808 words
with target as (
  select
    p.id as page_id,
    coalesce((
      select max(s.sort_order)
      from public.ielts_playbook_page_sections s
      where s.page_id = p.id
        and s.id <> all (array[md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:1')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:2')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:3')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:4')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:5')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:6')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:7')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:8')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:9')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:10')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:11')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:12')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:13')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:14')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:15')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:16')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:17')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:18')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:19')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:20')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:21')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:22')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:23')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-listening-traps-premium:24')::uuid])
    ), 0) as base_order
  from public.ielts_playbook_pages p
  where p.locale_code = 'en'
    and p.slug = 'guide-listening-traps-premium'
),
seed(n, block_type, heading, content) as (
  values
    (1, 'rich_text', $ipb$Advanced Lab: How to Use This Section$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Everything above this point explains the traps. This Advanced Lab is where you train against them under realistic pressure. It assumes you already know the four-part structure of IELTS Listening (40 questions, one recording per part, heard once) and that you can name the common trap types. If you cannot yet name them, go back and read the trap sections first; the Lab will feel confusing otherwise.\n\nThe Lab is built in four layers:\n\n1. **Precision techniques** – step-by-step methods for the moments where band 6.5 candidates usually lose marks: the pre-listening scan, live tracking, distractor dissection and answer transfer.\n2. **Worked examples** – short original transcript excerpts with the question, the evidence, the reasoning, and an explanation of why each tempting wrong answer fails.\n3. **Timed drills** – three drills with stated time limits. The answers are in a separate section so that you can attempt each drill honestly first.\n4. **Decision rules and repair planning** – if/then rules for live decisions, an error-diagnosis table, and a 14-day advanced repair schedule.\n\nWork through the Lab with a notebook open. Every time a technique surprises you, write one sentence about why. Those sentences become your personal rulebook, and a personal rulebook is far more useful in the exam room than anything you can memorise from a page."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (2, 'rich_text', $ipb$What Separates a Band 7 Listener From a Band 8 Listener$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"At band 6 to 6.5, most lost marks come from understanding problems: the candidate did not catch the word, or lost the thread. At band 7 to 8, understanding is usually good enough, and the lost marks come from **control** problems:\n\n- writing the first plausible word instead of the final confirmed word;\n- a spelling slip in a word that was heard perfectly;\n- breaking the word limit by adding an article or a second noun;\n- choosing a multiple-choice option because its words were heard, not because its meaning was confirmed;\n- losing one question and then losing the next two while searching for it;\n- transferring answers carelessly at the end.\n\nThe practical consequence is important: at the higher bands, **more listening practice alone gives diminishing returns**. What moves you is deliberate control of the answer-making process. Every technique in this Lab is about control, not about hearing more.\n\n### A useful self-test\n\nTake your last two practice tests and sort every wrong answer into one of two piles: \"I did not understand it\" and \"I understood it but wrote or chose the wrong thing\". If the second pile is the larger one, this Lab is exactly where your next band is hiding."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (3, 'rich_text', $ipb$Technique 1: The Three-Pass Pre-Listening Scan$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"You are given a short reading time before each part (and before each section of a part). Most candidates use it to read the questions once. Strong candidates run three fast passes.\n\n### Pass 1 – Grammar of the gap (about a third of the time)\n\nFor each gap, decide what **kind** of word fits: noun or verb, singular or plural, a number, a date, a name, an adjective. Write a tiny symbol next to it: *n*, *pl*, *#*, *£*, *date*, *adj*. This alone prevents a large share of plural and word-form mistakes.\n\n### Pass 2 – Prediction of meaning\n\nFor each gap, imagine one or two realistic answers. If the form says \"Parking available at: ______\", you might predict \"rear entrance\" or \"Mill Street\". You are not trying to be right; you are tuning your ear to the right category of word.\n\n### Pass 3 – Anchors\n\nUnderline one **anchor** per question: a word that is unlikely to be paraphrased, such as a name, a day, a number or a heading on the form. Anchors tell you where you are in the recording. Content words in the question are often paraphrased, so do not rely on them as anchors.\n\n### Why three passes and not one?\n\nOne slow read gives you a vague sense of the topic. Three fast passes give you a grammar check, a sound target and a map. In practice the three passes take no longer than one careful read once you have trained them for a week."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (4, 'rich_text', $ipb$Technique 2: Live Tracking With Two Questions in View$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"The single most damaging habit at band 7 is **tunnel listening**: focusing so hard on question 14 that question 15 passes unnoticed.\n\nThe fix is to keep two questions \"live\" at all times:\n\n1. Your eyes rest on the current question.\n2. Your peripheral attention sits on the next anchor.\n3. When you hear the next anchor, you move on immediately, even if the current gap is still empty.\n\n### The rule of the empty gap\n\nIf you hear the anchor for question 15 and question 14 is still blank, **write a mark in the margin and move on**. A blank gap costs one mark. Searching for it costs one mark plus however many you miss while searching. You can often reconstruct the missed answer later from the grammar and context you prepared in the scan.\n\n### Training drill\n\nPlay any recording and, without answering anything, simply tap the desk each time you hear an anchor word. If you cannot tap every anchor, your tracking is the problem, not your vocabulary."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (5, 'rich_text', $ipb$Technique 3: Distractor Anatomy$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"A distractor is not random noise. It almost always follows one of a small number of shapes. When you learn the shapes, you start hearing them coming.\n\n| Shape | What you hear | What it does to you |\n|---|---|---|\n| Self-correction | \"It's on the fourth... sorry, the fifth of May\" | Tempts you to keep the first value |\n| Rejected option | \"We thought about the coach, but it was too slow\" | Mentions an option that is ruled out |\n| Changed plan | \"Originally Tuesday, but now it'll be Thursday\" | First value was true once, not now |\n| Second speaker overrides | \"So, Monday?\" / \"No, Wednesday suits me better\" | One speaker proposes, the other decides |\n| Category confusion | A price for adults, then a price for students | Right type of answer, wrong row |\n| General then specific | \"Somewhere in the north... actually Hartley Road\" | The vague answer is not the answer |\n\n### The listening principle behind every shape\n\nThe answer is the **last confirmed version** of the information for the **exact person or category** in the question. Train yourself to wait for confirmation words: *so*, *then*, *in the end*, *we've decided*, *that's settled*, *let's go with*.\n\n### A caution\n\nNot every early mention is a distractor. Sometimes the first answer is simply the answer. Do not develop a superstition that the first thing you hear must be wrong. Instead, hold the first version lightly and stay alert for a change until the next anchor arrives."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (6, 'rich_text', $ipb$Technique 4: Word Limits and Spelling Under Pressure$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Instructions such as **\"Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER\"** are strict. An answer that exceeds the limit is marked wrong even if the meaning is perfect.\n\n### Reading the limit precisely\n\n- \"NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER\" allows: one word; two words; a number; one word and a number; two words and a number.\n- \"ONE WORD ONLY\" allows exactly one word, so \"a library\" is wrong where \"library\" is right.\n- Hyphenated words are generally treated as one word, but do not build answers that depend on that; if a natural answer fits without a hyphen problem, prefer it.\n- Numbers can be written as digits, and digits are safer than words because they avoid spelling errors.\n\n### The three-second spelling check\n\nWhen you write a word you have heard clearly, give it three seconds before moving on:\n\n1. Is it singular or plural, and does the grammar of the gap agree?\n2. Are there double letters you may have collapsed (*accommodation*, *committee*, *necessary*)?\n3. If it was spelled aloud, did you write every letter in the order given?\n\n### Spelled-out names and codes\n\nWhen a speaker spells a name or a postcode, write it letter by letter **as you hear it**, then read it back silently once. Common confusions in fast speech include A/E/I, G/J, M/N, B/P/V and S/F. Practise these pairs deliberately if you know they trouble you."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (7, 'rich_text', $ipb$Technique 5: Multiple-Choice Elimination in Real Time$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Multiple-choice questions punish candidates who match words instead of meanings. All three or four options are usually mentioned in some way, so hearing a word from an option proves very little.\n\n### The four-label method\n\nDuring the scan, reduce each option to a two- or three-word meaning label in the margin. While listening, mark each option with one of four symbols:\n\n- **✓** confirmed as true for the question asked;\n- **✗** clearly rejected or contradicted;\n- **~** mentioned, but not as the answer (for example, true of someone else, or true in the past);\n- **?** not yet heard.\n\nChoose the option that ends with **✓**. If you end with two **~** marks and no **✓**, choose the one most closely tied to the exact question focus (the reason, the main concern, the speaker's preference) rather than the one that was mentioned more often.\n\n### Questions with two answers from five options\n\n\"Choose TWO letters\" questions often mention all five options. Keep a running tally and only commit at the end of the relevant stretch. The order in which you write the two letters does not normally matter, but each letter must be one of the correct pair."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (8, 'rich_text', $ipb$Technique 6: Map and Plan Orientation Without Panic$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Map and plan questions feel difficult because they combine listening with spatial reasoning. The solution is to do the spatial reasoning **before** the recording starts.\n\n### Before you listen\n\n1. Find the starting point the speaker is likely to use: an entrance, a \"You are here\" marker, a reception desk.\n2. Note fixed features: a lake, a car park, a corridor, the main road.\n3. Label directions on the plan yourself if they are not marked: write *left*, *right*, *top* and *bottom* from the viewpoint of someone standing at the entrance.\n4. Look at which labelled locations are still free and how far apart they are.\n\n### While you listen\n\nMove your pen along the route as the speaker describes it. Never jump ahead to guess the destination. Listen for position language: *opposite*, *beyond*, *at the far end*, *just before you reach*, *on the corner of*, *between*, *alongside*.\n\n### The orientation trap\n\nSpeakers sometimes describe a location from a different viewpoint (\"if you're coming in from the garden side...\"). When this happens, physically rotate the paper or your mental image so that your viewpoint matches theirs. A rotated map is far easier than a mental translation under time pressure."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (9, 'rich_text', $ipb$Technique 7: Matching Questions and Signposting$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Matching tasks (for example, matching people to opinions, or stages to features) often follow the order of the questions but present the options in a mixed order. Signposting language is your best tool here.\n\n### Signposts to listen for\n\n- **Sequencing:** *first of all*, *moving on to*, *the next stage*, *finally*.\n- **Contrast:** *however*, *on the other hand*, *that said*, *whereas*.\n- **Emphasis:** *what really matters is*, *the key point*, *above all*.\n- **Summary:** *so overall*, *to sum up*, *the bottom line is*.\n\nIn Part 4 especially, a lecturer will usually announce structure (\"I'll look at three factors\"). Write the number down. If you have only filled two gaps by the time the speaker says \"finally\", you know a gap is missing and can mark it.\n\n### Matching method\n\nBefore listening, read the option list and write a short meaning label beside each option. During the recording, write option letters lightly next to questions as soon as you hear a match, then confirm or change them as the speaker continues. Options are often used once only, but check the instructions: some matching tasks allow an option to be used more than once."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (10, 'rich_text', $ipb$Computer vs Paper: Handling Your Answers$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"The answer-handling rules differ depending on how you take the test, so train in the format you will actually sit. Check the current rules with your test centre or the official IELTS sources before test day, because operational details can change.\n\n### Paper-based IELTS\n\n- You write answers on the question paper while listening.\n- At the end, you get **10 minutes to transfer** answers to the answer sheet.\n- Use the transfer time actively: check spelling, plurals, word limits and capital letters; fill any blanks with your best grammatically correct guess.\n- Transfer in blocks of ten and check the question numbers at each block, so that one skipped line does not shift every later answer.\n\n### Computer-delivered IELTS\n\n- You type answers directly into the boxes during the recording.\n- There is **no 10-minute transfer period**; you have about **2 minutes at the end to check** your answers.\n- Typing speed matters. If you type slowly, write a short note on the provided paper and type it in a pause.\n- Use the check time for blanks first, then spelling.\n\n### The shared rule\n\nIn both formats, there is no penalty for a wrong answer. **Never leave a gap empty.** A grammatically correct guess has a chance; a blank has none."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (11, 'callout', $ipb$The Last-Confirmed Rule$ipb$, $ipb${"label":"Rule to keep","body":"The answer is the last confirmed version of the information, for the exact person or category in the question, written within the word limit, spelled exactly. Every one of those four conditions must be true. Most band 7 errors fail exactly one of them."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (12, 'rich_text', $ipb$Worked Example 1: Correction Inside a Booking Call (Part 1 Style)$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Question:** Course start date: ______\n\n**Original transcript excerpt:**\n\n> **Receptionist:** The beginners' pottery course normally starts on the third of October.\n> **Caller:** Oh, I'm away that week.\n> **Receptionist:** Then you're in luck, because this term we've pushed everything back a week, so the first session is now on the tenth. The intermediate group still begins on the third, though.\n\n**Evidence:** \"the first session is now on the tenth\".\n\n**Reasoning:** The question is about the course the caller is booking (beginners). The word *normally* signals a default that may not apply. *Now* signals the updated fact.\n\n**Why the tempting wrong answers fail:**\n\n- **3 October** – heard first, and heard twice, but it is the old date for beginners and the current date for a different group (intermediate). This is a combined changed-plan and category trap.\n- **Any date \"a week\" before or after** – candidates who do mental arithmetic instead of listening for the stated date sometimes write the seventeenth. The speaker states the date directly, so arithmetic is unnecessary.\n\n**Correct answer:** 10 October (or 10th October / 10 Oct, written within the limit)."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (13, 'rich_text', $ipb$Worked Example 2: Option Elimination in a Tutorial (Part 3 Style)$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Question:** What is the students' main concern about the survey?\nA the number of questions  B the choice of participants  C the time available to analyse results\n\n**Original transcript excerpt:**\n\n> **Maya:** I know the questionnaire is a bit long, but people seem happy to finish it.\n> **Tom:** True. What bothers me more is that we only asked people at the station in the morning.\n> **Maya:** Exactly, they're nearly all commuters, so it doesn't represent the town at all. We've got plenty of weeks left to crunch the numbers, so that's not the issue.\n\n**Evidence:** \"What bothers me more is that we only asked people at the station...\" followed by agreement: \"Exactly... it doesn't represent the town\".\n\n**Reasoning:** The question asks for the *main* concern shared by the students. The phrase \"what bothers me more\" ranks one concern above another, and Maya's agreement makes it shared.\n\n**Why the tempting wrong answers fail:**\n\n- **A** – the length is mentioned first, but Maya immediately reduces its importance (\"people seem happy to finish it\"). Mark it **~**.\n- **C** – the analysis time is mentioned with a word associated with time pressure (\"crunch the numbers\"), but it is explicitly dismissed (\"that's not the issue\"). Mark it **✗**.\n\n**Correct answer:** B."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (14, 'rich_text', $ipb$Worked Example 3: Lecture Note Completion With a Word Limit (Part 4 Style)$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Question:** Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.\nEarly lighthouses: fires were burned on ______ to guide ships.\n\n**Original transcript excerpt:**\n\n> Long before glass lenses, coastal communities simply lit fires. At first these were built on the beach itself, but that was hardly visible from a distance, so people moved them up onto high cliffs, where the light carried much further out to sea.\n\n**Evidence:** \"moved them up onto high cliffs, where the light carried much further\".\n\n**Reasoning:** The lecture describes a change. The question describes the guiding method that worked, which is the improved version. \"Guide ships\" is paraphrased by \"the light carried much further out to sea\".\n\n**Why the tempting wrong answers fail:**\n\n- **the beach** – heard first, but described as \"hardly visible\", so it was not effective guidance; it is also an early-version trap.\n- **high coastal cliffs** – three words; it breaks the limit even though the meaning is close.\n- **cliff** – singular; the speaker says \"cliffs\" and the grammar \"on ______\" accepts either, but you should write what you hear. Markers expect the form used in the recording when it fits the gap.\n\n**Correct answer:** high cliffs (or cliffs)."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (15, 'rich_text', $ipb$Timed Drill A: Form Completion (Time Limit: 4 Minutes)$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Instructions:** Read the transcript below once at normal reading speed, as though you were hearing it, then cover it and complete the form. Allow **1 minute** to read the form first and **3 minutes** for the transcript and answers. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER.\n\n**Form:**\n\n1. Name of club: ______ Cycling Club\n2. Meeting point: outside the ______\n3. Day of weekly ride: ______\n4. Membership fee for students: £______ per year\n5. Item every member must bring: a ______\n\n**Original transcript:**\n\n> **Woman:** So you're interested in joining. We're the Ferndale Cycling Club, that's F-E-R-N-D-A-L-E. We used to gather at the town hall, but since the roadworks we meet outside the library instead. Rides were on Saturdays last year; this year we've switched to Sundays because the roads are quieter. Full membership is forty pounds a year, students pay twenty-five, and under-twelves ride free with a parent. Oh, and a helmet is compulsory. A repair kit is useful, but nobody will turn you away without one.\n\nCheck your answers in the Answers section before reading on."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (16, 'rich_text', $ipb$Timed Drill B: Multiple Choice and Matching (Time Limit: 5 Minutes)$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Instructions:** Allow **1 minute** to read the questions, then **4 minutes** for the transcript and answers.\n\n**Questions 6–7:** Choose the correct letter.\n\n6. Why did the speaker change her research topic?\nA Her supervisor suggested it. B She found more data on the new topic. C The original topic had already been studied.\n\n7. What does she say about the interviews?\nA They took longer than planned. B They produced unexpected results. C They were difficult to arrange.\n\n**Questions 8–10:** Match each source to the comment made about it. Choose from: A reliable but dated, B detailed but biased, C recent but limited.\n\n8. the council report  9. the local newspaper  10. the online survey\n\n**Original transcript:**\n\n> I began by looking at river pollution, and my supervisor was keen for me to carry on with it, but then I discovered that three theses in our department had covered it already, so I turned to urban noise instead, even though there's actually less published data on noise. The interviews went smoothly to set up, and they finished on schedule, but what surprised me was how many residents said traffic noise didn't bother them at all. For sources, the council report is careful and trustworthy, though it's from more than a decade ago. The local newspaper gives a lot of detail, but it's clearly campaigning against the new road. The online survey is only a few months old, which is great, but fewer than forty people answered it.\n\nCheck the Answers section before reading on."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (17, 'rich_text', $ipb$Timed Drill C: Map Labelling (Time Limit: 4 Minutes)$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Instructions:** Draw this simple plan on paper first (allow **1 minute**). Then read the transcript and label the plan (allow **3 minutes**).\n\n**Plan:** A rectangular park. The main gate is in the middle of the bottom (south) edge. A path runs straight north from the gate to a fountain in the centre. Four unlabelled buildings, marked A, B, C and D, stand at the four corners: A top-left, B top-right, C bottom-right, D bottom-left.\n\n**Label:** 11. café  12. toilets  13. playground\n\n**Original transcript:**\n\n> As you come through the main gate, turn right and follow the low wall; the first building you reach, in the corner, is the café. If you keep going and head up the eastern side towards the top of the park, you'll find the toilets in that corner. The playground isn't next to either of them: it's diagonally across the park from the café, in the far corner on the western side.\n\nCheck the Answers section before reading on."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (18, 'rich_text', $ipb$Answers: Timed Drills A, B and C$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"### Drill A\n\n1. **Ferndale** – spelled aloud; check every letter.\n2. **library** – \"town hall\" is the old meeting point (changed-plan trap).\n3. **Sunday** (or Sundays) – Saturday was last year.\n4. **25** – £40 is full membership; free is for under-twelves (category trap). Writing \"25\" as digits avoids spelling risk.\n5. **helmet** – \"repair kit\" is useful but not required; \"every member must bring\" matches \"compulsory\".\n\n### Drill B\n\n6. **C** – the supervisor is mentioned, so A is tempting, but she wanted the speaker to continue with the original topic. B is contradicted: there is less data on noise. The reason is that the topic had already been covered.\n7. **B** – A is contradicted (\"finished on schedule\") and C is contradicted (\"went smoothly to set up\"). The surprise in the residents' answers is the unexpected result.\n8. **A** – \"careful and trustworthy\" but \"more than a decade ago\".\n9. **B** – \"a lot of detail\" but \"campaigning\".\n10. **C** – \"a few months old\" but few respondents.\n\n### Drill C\n\n11. **C** (bottom-right) – \"turn right\" from a south-facing gate means east; the first corner reached is the south-east corner.\n12. **B** (top-right) – continuing up the eastern side to the top.\n13. **A** (top-left) – diagonally opposite the bottom-right café, on the western side.\n\n### Scoring yourself honestly\n\nCount any answer with a spelling error or an extra word as wrong, exactly as an examiner would. If you lost marks in Drill C, practise orientation; if in Drill A, practise changed-plan listening; if in Drill B, practise the four-label method."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (19, 'rich_text', $ipb$Decision Rules for the Live Test$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Use these rules as automatic responses. The point of a rule is that you do not need to think about it under pressure.\n\n- **If** you hear the next question's anchor and the current gap is empty, **then** mark it and move on immediately.\n- **If** a speaker uses *actually*, *sorry*, *I mean*, *no, wait* or *now*, **then** expect the previous value to be replaced.\n- **If** one speaker proposes and another responds, **then** wait for the response before writing.\n- **If** your answer has more words than the limit, **then** remove articles and adjectives first, and keep the core noun.\n- **If** two multiple-choice options both sound possible, **then** choose the one that answers the exact question word (*main*, *first*, *most*, *why*), not the one mentioned more often.\n- **If** you lose your place completely, **then** stop searching backwards, read the next two anchors, and rejoin there.\n- **If** you are unsure of a spelling at transfer time, **then** write the most standard spelling you know; never leave it blank.\n- **If** a number could be written as a word or digits, **then** use digits."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (20, 'rich_text', $ipb$Error-Diagnosis Table$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Use this table after every practice test. Find the symptom, confirm the likely cause, and apply the fix for at least a week before judging it.\n\n| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Right word, marked wrong | Spelling, plural or word-limit slip | Three-second check; spelling list of your own errors |\n| Wrote the first value heard | Not waiting for confirmation | Listen for *now*, *in the end*, *actually* before committing |\n| Missed two or three in a row | Tunnel listening on one gap | Two-questions-in-view tracking; empty-gap rule |\n| Multiple choice errors despite \"hearing\" the option | Word matching, not meaning matching | Four-label method (✓ ✗ ~ ?) |\n| Map questions mostly wrong | Orientation not set before audio | Label directions in the scan; rotate the plan |\n| Part 4 gaps blank | Lost structure of the lecture | Note the announced number of points; follow signposts |\n| Answers shifted on answer sheet | Careless paper transfer | Transfer in blocks of ten; check numbering each block |\n| Good scores at home, worse in mocks | Practising untimed or with pauses | Full-length timed practice, no replays |"}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (21, 'rich_text', $ipb$The 14-Day Advanced Repair Schedule$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"This schedule assumes about 45 to 60 minutes per day. Use original recordings from reliable IELTS practice material; never replay during the timed portion.\n\n| Day | Focus | Task |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Baseline | One full timed test; sort errors into \"understanding\" and \"control\" |\n| 2 | Scan | Three-pass scan on four forms; no audio, check your predictions afterwards |\n| 3 | Tracking | Anchor-tapping on two recordings; then answer one part |\n| 4 | Distractors | Collect ten correction and changed-plan moments in a notebook |\n| 5 | Word limits | Rewrite twenty of your past answers to fit strict limits |\n| 6 | Spelling | Build a list of your misspelled words; test yourself twice |\n| 7 | Review | One timed Parts 1–2; compare error types with Day 1 |\n| 8 | Multiple choice | Four-label method on three Part 3 recordings |\n| 9 | Maps | Two map tasks; set orientation before each one |\n| 10 | Matching | Signpost hunt on two Part 4 lectures; then answer the questions |\n| 11 | Format | Practise answer handling in your test format (paper transfer or computer check) |\n| 12 | Pressure | Full timed test in exam conditions |\n| 13 | Repair | Re-do only the question types where you lost the most marks |\n| 14 | Consolidation | Final timed test; update your personal rulebook |\n\n### How to judge progress\n\nCompare the **mix** of errors, not only the score. If \"control\" errors are shrinking, the method is working even if one test score dips. Scores naturally vary between practice tests."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (22, 'rich_text', $ipb$Building Your Personal Rulebook$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Generic advice is useful once. Your own rules are useful every time. After each practice test, write one rule in this form:\n\n> **When** [situation], **I tend to** [error], **so I will** [specific action].\n\nFor example: \"When a speaker spells a street name, I tend to drop double letters, so I will read the spelling back silently once before moving on.\"\n\nKeep the rulebook short; ten precise rules beat fifty vague ones. Read it the evening before a full practice test and again before the real exam. Delete rules once the error disappears for three tests in a row, so that the list always reflects your current weaknesses."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (23, 'faq', $ipb$Advanced Lab FAQ$ipb$, $ipb${"items":[{"question":"Should I write answers in capital letters?","answer":"Capitals are commonly accepted and can make handwriting clearer on paper, but check the current instructions for your test. Whatever you choose, be consistent and make sure every letter is legible."},{"question":"Is it worth predicting answers if my predictions are often wrong?","answer":"Yes. The value of prediction is in tuning your attention to the right type of word, not in guessing the exact answer. A wrong prediction of the right category still helps you hear the real answer."},{"question":"Do accents matter for the higher bands?","answer":"The test uses a range of accents. At higher bands, accent difficulty is usually smaller than control errors, but if one accent consistently causes trouble, add a few recordings in that accent to your weekly practice."},{"question":"Can I change an answer after the recording moves on?","answer":"You can, but only if you have a specific reason, such as a later correction you heard. Do not change answers based on vague doubt; first instincts backed by evidence are usually more reliable than late second guesses."},{"question":"How many full tests should I do in two weeks?","answer":"Quality matters more than quantity. Two or three full timed tests in two weeks, each followed by careful error analysis, are usually more useful than a test every day with no review."}]}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (24, 'checklist', $ipb$Advanced Lab Final Checklist$ipb$, $ipb${"items":["I run a three-pass scan: grammar of the gap, meaning prediction, anchors.","I keep two questions in view and use the empty-gap rule without hesitation.","I can name the distractor shapes and wait for the last confirmed version.","I read every word limit literally and remove extra words before moving on.","I use digits for numbers and check plurals and double letters.","I mark multiple-choice options with ✓, ✗, ~ and ? instead of matching words.","I set map orientation before the audio starts and rotate the plan if needed.","I follow signposts and note the number of points a lecturer announces.","I know how answers are handled in my format: 10 minutes to transfer on paper, about 2 minutes to check on computer.","I never leave a gap empty.","I sort every practice error into understanding or control and apply the diagnosis table.","I keep a short personal rulebook and update it after each test."]}$ipb$::jsonb)
)
insert into public.ielts_playbook_page_sections (id, page_id, block_type, heading, status, sort_order, content, metadata)
select
  md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:' || 'guide-listening-traps-premium' || ':' || seed.n)::uuid,
  target.page_id,
  seed.block_type,
  seed.heading,
  'published',
  target.base_order + seed.n,
  seed.content,
  jsonb_build_object(
    'seeded_by', '20261026120100_ielts_playbook_premium_advanced_labs',
    'pack', 'advanced_lab',
    'content_revision', 'premium-advanced-lab-2026-10',
    'source_file', 'content/ielts-playbook/premium-guides/guide-listening-traps-premium.json'
  )
from target
cross join seed
on conflict (id) do nothing;

-- guide-reading-paraphrase-premium: 23 sections, 4,153 words
with target as (
  select
    p.id as page_id,
    coalesce((
      select max(s.sort_order)
      from public.ielts_playbook_page_sections s
      where s.page_id = p.id
        and s.id <> all (array[md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:1')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:2')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:3')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:4')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:5')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:6')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:7')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:8')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:9')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:10')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:11')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:12')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:13')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:14')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:15')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:16')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:17')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:18')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:19')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:20')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:21')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:22')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-reading-paraphrase-premium:23')::uuid])
    ), 0) as base_order
  from public.ielts_playbook_pages p
  where p.locale_code = 'en'
    and p.slug = 'guide-reading-paraphrase-premium'
),
seed(n, block_type, heading, content) as (
  values
    (1, 'rich_text', $ipb$Advanced Lab: How to Use This Section$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"The sections above explain how paraphrase works and why keyword hunting fails. This Advanced Lab trains the decisions that still cost marks once you understand those ideas: boundary cases in True/False/Not Given, headings that all look plausible, summaries with and without a word box, and the time decisions that decide whether you finish Passage 3.\n\nThe format facts you should hold in mind throughout: IELTS Reading lasts **60 minutes**, contains **three passages** and **40 questions**, and there is no extra transfer time in the paper test, so answers must reach the answer sheet within the hour. On computer, answers are entered directly as you go.\n\nThe Lab has four layers:\n\n1. **Precision techniques** with step-by-step methods.\n2. **Worked examples** using short original passages, showing the evidence, the reasoning and why each tempting wrong answer fails.\n3. **Timed drills** with stated time limits and a separate Answers section.\n4. **Decision rules, an error-diagnosis table and a 14-day repair schedule.**\n\nDo every drill before reading its answers. Reading answers first trains recognition, not judgement, and the exam rewards judgement."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (2, 'rich_text', $ipb$Where Band 7 and Band 8 Readers Actually Lose Marks$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"At around band 6, candidates lose marks because they cannot locate information or do not understand it. Around band 7 to 8, location and understanding are mostly reliable, and lost marks come from **precision** errors:\n\n- treating a partial match as a full match in True/False/Not Given;\n- choosing a heading that describes one example in the paragraph rather than the paragraph's main purpose;\n- picking a summary word that means the right thing but does not fit the grammar of the gap;\n- reading a cautious statement (\"may\", \"some researchers suggest\") as a definite claim;\n- spending too long on one stubborn question and rushing the final passage.\n\nThe pattern is that high-band errors are **judgement errors under time pressure**. That is good news: judgement can be trained with clear rules and repeated feedback."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (3, 'rich_text', $ipb$Technique 1: Statement Dissection for TFNG and YNNG$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Before looking at the passage, break each statement into its **claim components**. Most statements contain more than one testable element.\n\n### The dissection method\n\n1. **Subject** – who or what is the statement about?\n2. **Claim** – what does it say about the subject?\n3. **Qualifiers** – words that narrow the claim: *all*, *only*, *always*, *first*, *most*, *never*, *some*, *increasingly*.\n4. **Comparison or time** – *more than*, *earlier*, *since*, *before*, *after*.\n\nExample statement: *\"The museum was the first in the region to charge no entry fee.\"*\n\n- Subject: the museum\n- Claim: charged no entry fee\n- Qualifier: first in the region\n\nTo be **True**, the passage must support every component, including *first in the region*. If the passage says only that the museum charged no fee, the statement is **Not Given**, because the \"first\" component has no evidence. If the passage says another museum had done so earlier, it is **False**.\n\n### Why this matters\n\nMost TFNG errors at higher bands happen because one qualifier was not checked. Dissection forces you to check each part separately."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (4, 'rich_text', $ipb$Technique 2: Boundary Cases Between False and Not Given$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"The hardest TFNG decisions sit on the boundary between False and Not Given. Use this test:\n\n> **Does the passage say something that cannot be true at the same time as the statement?**\n\n- If **yes**, the answer is **False**. The passage contradicts the statement.\n- If the passage is **silent** on the point, or says something related but compatible, the answer is **Not Given**.\n\n### Common boundary patterns\n\n| Statement says | Passage says | Answer |\n|---|---|---|\n| X increased | X stayed the same | False |\n| X increased | X was measured | Not Given |\n| Most people preferred A | People preferred B | False |\n| Most people preferred A | Some people preferred A | Not Given |\n| X was invented in Italy | X was first sold in Italy | Not Given |\n| X always happens | X happens in some cases | False (if \"some\" implies \"not always\" in context), otherwise Not Given |\n\nThe last row is the classic trap. \"Some\" does not automatically contradict \"always\". Ask whether the passage clearly signals exceptions (\"but not in every case\", \"only occasionally\"). If it does, False; if it does not, Not Given.\n\n### YNNG: the writer's view\n\nFor Yes/No/Not Given, replace \"the passage says\" with \"the writer believes\". A fact reported by the writer is not the same as the writer's opinion. If the writer quotes someone else's view without agreeing or disagreeing, the writer's own position is usually **Not Given**."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (5, 'rich_text', $ipb$Technique 3: Headings by Function, Not Topic$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Paragraph headings fail when candidates match **topic** words. Many headings share the paragraph's topic; only one matches its **function**.\n\n### The function-first method\n\n1. Read the paragraph's first two sentences and its last sentence.\n2. Ask: what is the paragraph **doing**? Explaining a cause, describing a problem, comparing two views, giving a solution, describing a process, predicting the future?\n3. Write a three-word function label in the margin: *cause of decline*, *proposed solution*, *limits of evidence*.\n4. Only now look at the headings, and match the label to the heading.\n\n### Example-heading trap\n\nA paragraph about water shortages that mentions one city as an example should not be matched with a heading about that city. The example supports the paragraph's point; it is not the point.\n\n### Two-heading conflict\n\nIf two headings fit, check which one covers **the whole paragraph**. The correct heading is usually the one that would still be true if you deleted the paragraph's examples."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (6, 'rich_text', $ipb$Technique 4: Summary Completion With and Without a Word Box$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Summary completion has two forms, and they need different methods.\n\n### Without a word box (words from the passage)\n\n- Read the instruction limit literally, for example \"NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage\".\n- Locate the paragraph that the summary covers; summaries usually follow the order of the passage within the relevant section.\n- Copy the words **exactly as spelled in the passage**. Do not change word forms.\n- Check grammar: the word must fit the gap as written in the passage.\n\n### With a word box (choose from a list)\n\n- The box words are often **paraphrases** of passage words, so the exact passage word may not appear.\n- First, predict the **type** of word each gap needs (noun, adjective, plural noun).\n- Eliminate box words that do not fit the grammar. This often removes half the options before you read the passage again.\n- Then choose by meaning. Beware of box words that are **opposites** of the correct answer; they are deliberately included.\n\n### Shared check\n\nRead the completed summary sentence aloud in your head. If it sounds ungrammatical, at least one answer is wrong."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (7, 'rich_text', $ipb$Technique 5: Matching Features and Sentence Endings$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"### Matching features (for example, researchers to findings)\n\n- Find every occurrence of each name **before** matching. Names are easy to scan for, and they may appear in more than one paragraph.\n- Underline the claim attached to each name.\n- Check whether options may be used more than once; read the instruction.\n- A name followed by \"however, critics argue\" means the finding after \"critics\" does not belong to that name.\n\n### Matching sentence endings\n\n- Treat each beginning and ending as one sentence; the combined sentence must be **true according to the passage** and **grammatically complete**.\n- First eliminate endings that do not fit grammatically with the beginning. This is fast and reliable.\n- Then check meaning in the passage. There are more endings than beginnings, so several endings are distractors that are true of something else in the passage.\n- Questions usually follow the order of the passage, which helps you locate each beginning."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (8, 'rich_text', $ipb$Technique 6: Inference vs Paraphrase$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Most IELTS Reading answers are **paraphrases**: the passage says the same thing in different words. Some questions, especially in multiple choice and YNNG, require a small **inference**: a conclusion that follows directly from what is stated.\n\n### Safe inference\n\nThe passage says: *\"Applications rose sharply after the fees were removed.\"*\nA safe inference: *Removing fees was followed by an increase in applications.*\n\n### Unsafe inference\n\nAn unsafe inference: *Fees were the main reason people did not apply before.* The passage does not state the cause; it states a sequence.\n\n### The rule\n\nAccept an inference only if a careful reader would say \"the passage clearly means this\". Reject any option that requires you to add **a cause, a motive, a comparison or a generalisation** that the passage does not provide. In the exam, unsafe inference is one of the most common ways that strong readers talk themselves into wrong answers."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (9, 'rich_text', $ipb$Technique 7: Time-Boxing the Three Passages$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Passages generally become more demanding as the test progresses, so equal time is rarely the best split. A practical starting plan:\n\n| Passage | Suggested time | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Passage 1 | about 17 minutes | Bank marks quickly and accurately |\n| Passage 2 | about 20 minutes | Steady pace; protect accuracy |\n| Passage 3 | about 23 minutes | Most demanding; needs the extra time |\n\nAdjust this split after a few timed tests, based on your own data.\n\n### Hard stops\n\nSet a **hard stop** for each passage. When it arrives, write your best answer for every remaining question in that passage and move on. A stubborn question is worth one mark, exactly the same as an easy question in Passage 3 that you might never reach.\n\n### The two-minute rule\n\nIf you have spent about two minutes on a single question without progress, guess sensibly, mark it, and move on. Return only if time remains at the end.\n\n### Paper test reminder\n\nOn paper there is no separate transfer time for Reading. Either write answers directly on the answer sheet as you go or transfer after each passage. Never leave all transfers to the last minutes."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (10, 'callout', $ipb$The Evidence Rule$ipb$, $ipb${"label":"Rule to keep","body":"Every answer must rest on a specific line of the passage that you could point to. If you cannot point to the line, you are answering from memory or from general knowledge, and general knowledge is exactly what IELTS Reading tests you to set aside."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (11, 'rich_text', $ipb$Worked Example 1: A TFNG Boundary Case$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Original passage excerpt:**\n\n> When the town of Elmsworth introduced a weekly farmers' market in 2015, local shopkeepers feared the competition. Within two years, however, several shops on the high street reported more customers on market days than on any other day of the week.\n\n**Statements:**\n\n1. Shopkeepers in Elmsworth were initially worried about the market.\n2. All shops on the high street benefited from the market.\n3. The market was the first of its kind in the region.\n\n**Reasoning and answers:**\n\n1. **True** – \"feared the competition\" paraphrases \"were worried\"; \"initially\" matches the time contrast created by \"however\" and \"within two years\".\n2. **Not Given** – the passage says \"several shops\" reported more customers. \"Several\" does not tell us about all shops, and the passage does not say that any shop did badly. Nothing contradicts \"all\", but nothing supports it either. Candidates often choose False here because \"several\" feels smaller than \"all\", but the passage never states that some shops failed to benefit.\n3. **Not Given** – the region and any other markets are never mentioned. The date 2015 tempts candidates to infer novelty, but a date is not a claim of being first."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (12, 'rich_text', $ipb$Worked Example 2: Choosing a Heading by Function$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Original paragraph:**\n\n> For decades, engineers assumed that taller sea walls were the only realistic defence against coastal flooding. Yet in several low-lying areas, restored salt marshes have absorbed storm surges more effectively than concrete, and at lower long-term cost. A pilot scheme on one estuary, for instance, recorded less damage to nearby farmland after the marsh was allowed to return.\n\n**Heading options:**\n\n- i The cost of building sea walls\n- ii A pilot scheme on an estuary\n- iii Natural defences challenging an old assumption\n- iv Why farmland floods\n\n**Reasoning:** The function of the paragraph is to present an alternative that challenges a long-held belief. The first sentence states the assumption; \"Yet\" introduces the challenge; the last sentence is an example.\n\n**Answer: iii.**\n\n**Why the tempting wrong answers fail:**\n\n- **i** – cost is mentioned only as a supporting detail (\"at lower long-term cost\").\n- **ii** – the pilot scheme is the example, introduced by \"for instance\". Matching the example is the most common heading error.\n- **iv** – farmland appears, but the paragraph does not explain causes of flooding."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (13, 'rich_text', $ipb$Worked Example 3: Summary Completion With a Word Box$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Original passage excerpt:**\n\n> Early clockmakers struggled with the effect of temperature on their mechanisms. Metal parts expanded in warm weather and contracted in the cold, so a clock that kept perfect time in winter might run slow by summer. The eventual solution combined two metals that expanded at different rates, so that their changes cancelled each other out.\n\n**Summary:** Clocks were once affected by changes in (1) ______. Warm conditions made metal parts (2) ______, which altered timekeeping. The answer was to use two metals whose changes (3) ______ each other.\n\n**Word box:** A humidity B temperature C shrink D enlarge E balanced F reinforced\n\n**Reasoning and answers:**\n\n1. **B temperature** – stated directly. *Humidity* (A) is a related weather word but is never mentioned.\n2. **D enlarge** – \"expanded\" is paraphrased by \"enlarge\". *Shrink* (C) is the deliberate opposite: it describes cold conditions, not warm ones.\n3. **E balanced** – \"cancelled each other out\" means the effects balanced each other. *Reinforced* (F) means the opposite: strengthened each other. Grammar check: \"whose changes balanced each other\" reads correctly."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (14, 'rich_text', $ipb$Timed Drill A: TFNG Precision (Time Limit: 5 Minutes)$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Instructions:** Read the passage and decide whether each statement is TRUE, FALSE or NOT GIVEN. Allow **5 minutes** in total.\n\n**Original passage:**\n\n> The Harlow Botanical Collection began as a private garden in the 1880s. Its founder, a retired sea captain, brought back seeds from voyages across the Pacific, although many failed to grow in the cooler climate. In 1923 the garden was handed to the city, which opened it to the public free of charge. Visitor numbers fell during the 1970s, when much of the glasshouse fell into disrepair, but a restoration completed in 2009 has made the tropical house the collection's most visited area. Today the gardens employ a team of botanists who exchange seeds with institutions in several countries.\n\n**Statements:**\n\n1. The founder's seeds all grew successfully.\n2. The city has always charged an entry fee.\n3. The glasshouse was damaged by a storm in the 1970s.\n4. The tropical house is currently the most popular part of the collection.\n5. The gardens exchange seeds with more countries than any other botanical collection.\n\nCheck the Answers section before reading on."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (15, 'rich_text', $ipb$Timed Drill B: Matching Headings (Time Limit: 6 Minutes)$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Instructions:** Choose the correct heading for each paragraph. There are more headings than paragraphs. Allow **6 minutes**.\n\n**Headings:** i An unexpected side effect ii How the scheme was funded iii Reasons for the original decision iv Plans to extend the idea v A comparison with older methods\n\n**Original paragraphs:**\n\n**Paragraph A:** City planners closed the central square to cars in 2018 after years of complaints about congestion and air quality. Businesses had argued that drivers were deterred from shopping in the centre by the difficulty of finding parking near the square.\n\n**Paragraph B:** What nobody predicted was the effect on the square's pigeons. With traffic gone, outdoor cafés multiplied, and the birds, attracted by dropped food, became so numerous that the council had to launch a cleaning campaign.\n\n**Paragraph C:** Encouraged by the overall results, the council is now consulting residents about closing two further streets, and a cycle route linking the square with the railway station is being designed.\n\nCheck the Answers section before reading on."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (16, 'rich_text', $ipb$Timed Drill C: Sentence Endings and Summary Without a Word Box (Time Limit: 6 Minutes)$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Instructions:** Allow **6 minutes** in total.\n\n**Original passage:**\n\n> Researchers studying honeybees have found that colonies make collective decisions about new nest sites through a process resembling a vote. Scout bees inspect possible sites and return to perform a dance that advertises their choice; the better the site, the longer the dance. Over time, more scouts visit the most advertised site, and once enough of them gather there, the swarm departs. Interestingly, scouts gradually reduce the length of their dances, which prevents the colony from becoming stuck on an early but inferior option.\n\n**Questions 6–7: Complete each sentence with the correct ending.**\n\n6. The length of a scout's dance\n7. The gradual shortening of dances\n\nEndings: A allows the colony to change its mind. B depends on the quality of the site. C is decided by the queen. D causes the swarm to leave immediately.\n\n**Questions 8–9: Complete the summary. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage.**\n\nScouts advertise a site by performing a (8) ______. The swarm leaves once enough scouts have gathered at the most (9) ______ site.\n\nCheck the Answers section before reading on."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (17, 'rich_text', $ipb$Answers: Timed Drills A, B and C$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"### Drill A\n\n1. **False** – \"many failed to grow\" contradicts \"all grew successfully\".\n2. **False** – the city opened the garden \"free of charge\" in 1923, which contradicts \"always charged\".\n3. **Not Given** – the glasshouse \"fell into disrepair\", but no cause is given. A storm is an unsafe inference.\n4. **True** – \"has made the tropical house the collection's most visited area\" plus \"Today\".\n5. **Not Given** – \"several countries\" is stated, but there is no comparison with other collections.\n\n### Drill B\n\n- **A – iii** – the paragraph explains why the square was closed (congestion, air quality). The business argument is part of the background, not a heading on its own.\n- **B – i** – \"What nobody predicted\" signals an unexpected side effect.\n- **C – iv** – closing more streets and a new cycle route are plans to extend the idea.\n- Headings **ii** and **v** are distractors: funding and older methods are never discussed.\n\n### Drill C\n\n6. **B** – \"the better the site, the longer the dance\".\n7. **A** – shortening dances \"prevents the colony from becoming stuck on an early but inferior option\", that is, it allows a change of mind. **D** is tempting but the swarm leaves when enough scouts gather, not because of shortening dances. **C** is never mentioned.\n8. **dance** – copy the passage word exactly; \"waggle dance\" or other additions are not in this passage.\n9. **advertised** – the passage says \"the most advertised site\". *Popular* would be a paraphrase and is not allowed when the instruction says \"from the passage\".\n\n### Scoring yourself honestly\n\nMark any answer with a spelling error, a changed word form or an extra word as wrong. If Drill A cost you marks, return to statement dissection; if Drill B, practise function labels; if Drill C, practise grammar-first elimination."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (18, 'rich_text', $ipb$Decision Rules for the Live Test$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"- **If** a statement contains *all*, *only*, *first*, *always* or *never*, **then** check that qualifier first; it is usually the deciding component.\n- **If** the passage could be true at the same time as the statement but does not confirm it, **then** the answer is Not Given, not False.\n- **If** the passage reports someone else's opinion, **then** do not treat it as the writer's view in YNNG.\n- **If** two headings both fit, **then** choose the one that remains true without the paragraph's examples.\n- **If** a summary has a word box, **then** eliminate by grammar before reading for meaning.\n- **If** a summary says \"from the passage\", **then** copy the exact form and spelling from the passage.\n- **If** a multiple-choice option needs a cause, motive or comparison not stated, **then** reject it.\n- **If** you have spent about two minutes on one question, **then** guess, mark it and move on.\n- **If** a passage's hard stop arrives, **then** fill every remaining answer for that passage before moving on."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (19, 'rich_text', $ipb$Error-Diagnosis Table$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Many False answers that should be Not Given | Treating \"not confirmed\" as \"contradicted\" | Apply the \"both true at once?\" test |\n| Many Not Given answers that should be False | Missing a contradiction hidden in a qualifier | Dissect statements; check qualifiers first |\n| Headings wrong despite good understanding | Matching topic words or examples | Function labels from first, second and last sentences |\n| Summary answers right in meaning, marked wrong | Changed word form or exceeded limit | Copy exactly; read the instruction limit literally |\n| Word-box answers often opposites | Reading too fast for direction of meaning | Check positive/negative direction before choosing |\n| Passage 3 unfinished | No hard stops; stuck on single questions | Time-box passages; two-minute rule |\n| Multiple choice errors on \"the writer suggests\" | Unsafe inference | Accept only what the passage clearly means |\n| Answers correct on question paper, wrong on sheet | Late or careless transfer (paper) | Transfer after each passage; check numbering |"}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (20, 'rich_text', $ipb$The 14-Day Advanced Repair Schedule$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"About 45 to 60 minutes a day, using reliable IELTS practice passages.\n\n| Day | Focus | Task |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Baseline | One full timed test; classify every error by question type and cause |\n| 2 | Dissection | Dissect twenty TFNG statements before reading the passages |\n| 3 | Boundaries | Fifteen False vs Not Given decisions; write the line of evidence for each |\n| 4 | Headings | Function labels for two passages before looking at the heading list |\n| 5 | Summaries | Two summaries with word boxes; grammar elimination first |\n| 6 | Summaries | Two summaries from the passage; exact copying and limits |\n| 7 | Review | One timed passage of each difficulty; compare errors with Day 1 |\n| 8 | Matching | Matching features and sentence endings on two passages |\n| 9 | Inference | Ten \"writer suggests\" questions; reject unsafe inferences in writing |\n| 10 | YNNG | One opinion-heavy passage; separate reported views from the writer's view |\n| 11 | Timing | Full test with hard stops; record time spent per passage |\n| 12 | Repair | Re-do the two weakest question types from Day 11 |\n| 13 | Pressure | Full timed test in exam conditions, including answer transfer if on paper |\n| 14 | Consolidation | Review the error log; write five personal rules |\n\n### Judging progress\n\nTrack your errors by **cause**, not only your total. A falling number of qualifier and inference errors shows the method is working, even if one test's total moves the other way. Practice scores naturally vary."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (21, 'rich_text', $ipb$Your Evidence Log$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"For one week, write the evidence line for every answer you give in practice, not just the wrong ones. It slows you down at first, but it builds the habit that matters most at the higher bands: answering from the text, not from impression.\n\nA simple format:\n\n| Q | Answer | Evidence line (first few words) | Confident? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 14 | NG | \"several shops reported...\" | Medium |\n\nAfter a week, look at the answers you marked \"Medium\" or \"Low\". Patterns appear quickly, for example, uncertainty clustered around qualifiers, or around headings in Passage 3. Those patterns tell you exactly where to aim the next week of practice."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (22, 'faq', $ipb$Advanced Lab FAQ$ipb$, $ipb${"items":[{"question":"Should I read the whole passage before the questions?","answer":"For most strong readers, a fast skim of the passage structure (first sentences of paragraphs) followed by the questions works best. A full careful read before the questions often costs too much time, especially in Passage 3."},{"question":"Do questions always follow the order of the passage?","answer":"Many question types usually follow passage order, such as TFNG and sentence completion, but headings and some matching tasks do not. Read the instructions and use order as a helpful pattern, not a guarantee."},{"question":"Is it acceptable to write T, F and NG instead of full words?","answer":"Abbreviations are commonly accepted, but follow the instructions printed on your test and check the current guidance from official IELTS sources. Be careful never to write Yes/No answers for a True/False question or the reverse."},{"question":"How do I stop overthinking Not Given?","answer":"Use one test only: can the statement and the passage both be true at the same time? If the passage is silent or compatible, it is Not Given. Stop there and move on."},{"question":"Does Academic or General Training change these methods?","answer":"The question types and decision rules are the same. The texts differ in source and style, so General Training candidates should also practise everyday and workplace texts, but the techniques in this Lab apply to both."}]}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (23, 'checklist', $ipb$Advanced Lab Final Checklist$ipb$, $ipb${"items":["I dissect statements into subject, claim, qualifiers and comparisons before reading.","I use the \"both true at once?\" test to separate False from Not Given.","I separate the writer's view from reported views in Yes/No/Not Given.","I write a function label for a paragraph before reading the heading list.","I never match a heading to a paragraph's example.","I eliminate word-box options by grammar first, then meaning.","I copy passage words exactly when the instruction says \"from the passage\".","I reject inferences that add a cause, motive, comparison or generalisation.","I time-box all three passages and respect my hard stops.","I use the two-minute rule on stubborn questions.","On paper, I transfer answers within the 60 minutes, passage by passage.","I keep an error log by cause and update my personal rules every week."]}$ipb$::jsonb)
)
insert into public.ielts_playbook_page_sections (id, page_id, block_type, heading, status, sort_order, content, metadata)
select
  md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:' || 'guide-reading-paraphrase-premium' || ':' || seed.n)::uuid,
  target.page_id,
  seed.block_type,
  seed.heading,
  'published',
  target.base_order + seed.n,
  seed.content,
  jsonb_build_object(
    'seeded_by', '20261026120100_ielts_playbook_premium_advanced_labs',
    'pack', 'advanced_lab',
    'content_revision', 'premium-advanced-lab-2026-10',
    'source_file', 'content/ielts-playbook/premium-guides/guide-reading-paraphrase-premium.json'
  )
from target
cross join seed
on conflict (id) do nothing;

-- guide-writing-task-2-premium: 24 sections, 6,083 words
with target as (
  select
    p.id as page_id,
    coalesce((
      select max(s.sort_order)
      from public.ielts_playbook_page_sections s
      where s.page_id = p.id
        and s.id <> all (array[md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:1')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:2')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:3')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:4')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:5')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:6')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:7')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:8')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:9')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:10')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:11')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:12')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:13')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:14')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:15')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:16')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:17')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:18')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:19')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:20')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:21')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:22')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:23')::uuid, md5('schood-ielts-pb-section:guide-writing-task-2-premium:24')::uuid])
    ), 0) as base_order
  from public.ielts_playbook_pages p
  where p.locale_code = 'en'
    and p.slug = 'guide-writing-task-2-premium'
),
seed(n, block_type, heading, content) as (
  values
    (1, 'rich_text', $ipb$Advanced Lab: How to Use This Section$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Everything above this point gives you the core Task 2 system: analyse the question, take a position, plan two ideas, develop them and check your work. This Advanced Lab assumes you can already do that reliably and are now aiming at Band 7 or above in Writing.\n\nThe lab is built around three kinds of work:\n\n- **Techniques** that change how you think about an essay before you write a word: choosing a structure for the question type, building a clear position, and developing ideas through mechanisms rather than lists.\n- **Worked examples** at two levels, a Band 7-style essay and a Band 8-style essay, each followed by a criterion-by-criterion commentary so you can see why the stronger essay is stronger.\n- **Timed drills** with answers, an error-diagnosis table and a 14-day repair schedule, so the ideas turn into habits under exam conditions.\n\nDo not read the lab in one sitting. Work through one technique, then do the matching drill, then return to your own recent essays and look for the same problem. Band movement in Writing comes from repeated, deliberate correction, not from reading more advice.\n\nA note on the band labels used here: the worked examples are written to illustrate typical features of essays at those levels. They are teaching models, not officially scored scripts, and no single essay guarantees a band."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (2, 'rich_text', $ipb$The Band 7 to Band 8 Shift in Task 2$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"At Band 6, the main question an examiner might ask is: *does this essay answer the task?* At Band 7 and above, the question becomes: *how well, how clearly and how precisely does it answer the task?* The shift is subtle, and it explains why many candidates feel stuck at 6.5 even though their English is strong.\n\n### What usually changes between 7 and 8\n\n- **Position.** A Band 7 essay has a clear position that is maintained. A Band 8 essay has a position that is not only clear but *qualified*: it shows where the argument applies and where it does not.\n- **Development.** A Band 7 essay extends its main ideas and supports them. A Band 8 essay develops them so that each step follows logically from the last, and the reader never has to fill a gap.\n- **Organisation.** At 7, paragraphing is logical and linking is generally effective, though it may be slightly mechanical. At 8, the reader hardly notices the linking because the ideas themselves are sequenced well.\n- **Vocabulary.** At 7, there is a good range with some awareness of style and collocation. At 8, word choice is precise: words are chosen because they say exactly what the writer means, not because they sound advanced.\n- **Grammar.** At 7, a variety of complex structures with frequent error-free sentences. At 8, the majority of sentences are error-free and complex structures serve the meaning.\n\n### The practical consequence\n\nYou do not reach Band 8 by adding more of anything. You reach it by removing vagueness: vague positions, vague examples, vague linking and vague words. Every technique in this lab is a way of replacing something general with something exact."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (3, 'rich_text', $ipb$Decision Rules: Choosing a Structure for Each Question Type$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Advanced writers do not use one structure for every essay. They read the question, identify what it actually demands, and pick a structure that answers every part. Use these if/then rules during your first three minutes.\n\n| If the question asks... | Then use... | Watch out for... |\n|---|---|---|\n| To what extent do you agree or disagree? | Clear position in the introduction; two body paragraphs supporting it, or one supporting and one conceding with a rebuttal | Sitting on the fence with no final view |\n| Discuss both views and give your opinion | One paragraph per view; your opinion stated in the introduction and confirmed in the conclusion | Forgetting your own opinion until the last line |\n| Do the advantages outweigh the disadvantages? | Weigh, do not list: one paragraph for each side, then a judgement on which is heavier and why | Writing \"there are many advantages and disadvantages\" with no verdict |\n| Why is this happening? Is it positive or negative? | One paragraph per question, in the order asked | Answering only the second question |\n| What are the problems and how can they be solved? | Problems paragraph, solutions paragraph, with each solution linked to a named problem | Generic solutions such as \"the government should do more\" |\n| A direct question with two parts | Answer each part explicitly, ideally in its own paragraph | Merging both parts so neither is fully answered |\n\n### Three further rules\n\n1. **If a question contains a qualifier** such as *always*, *only*, *the best way* or *all*, then your position should engage with that word. Agreeing that something is *a* good way is not the same as agreeing it is *the best* way.\n2. **If you can think of a strong counter-argument**, then include it and answer it. A conceded point that you rebut makes your position stronger, not weaker.\n3. **If you cannot decide your position within two minutes**, then choose the side you can explain most concretely. Examiners assess how well you argue, not which side you take."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (4, 'rich_text', $ipb$Advanced Technique: The Position Ladder$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Many Band 6 and 6.5 essays state a position that is technically clear but too broad to argue well. The position ladder is a way of moving from a broad opinion to a precise, arguable thesis in about one minute.\n\n### Step by step\n\n1. **Rung one: the raw opinion.** Write the simplest version. *Working from home is good.*\n2. **Rung two: add the condition.** Under what circumstances is it true? *Working from home is good for jobs that do not require physical presence.*\n3. **Rung three: add the reason.** Why? *...because it saves commuting time and allows focused work.*\n4. **Rung four: add the limit.** Where does your view stop? *...although it can weaken teamwork if it becomes permanent for everyone.*\n\n### The finished thesis\n\n*For roles that do not depend on physical presence, working from home is largely beneficial because it removes commuting time and allows uninterrupted work, although making it permanent for every employee risks weakening collaboration.*\n\n### Why this works\n\nThe thesis now tells the reader exactly what your body paragraphs will do: one paragraph on time and focus, one paragraph on the limit. Your conclusion simply returns to the same idea. This is what a clear position *throughout the response* looks like in practice: the introduction, the body and the conclusion all point in the same direction because they were built from the same sentence.\n\nDo not let the thesis grow beyond about 40 words. If it needs more, your argument is too complicated for 40 minutes."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (5, 'rich_text', $ipb$Advanced Technique: Mechanism-Based Development$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"The most common reason a strong candidate stays at Band 6.5 is under-developed ideas. The paragraph states a point, gives a quick example and moves on. The reader has to *assume* how the example proves the point. Mechanism-based development closes that gap.\n\n### The mechanism chain\n\nFor every main idea, write four links:\n\n1. **Claim:** what you believe.\n2. **Mechanism:** *how* or *why* it happens, explained step by step.\n3. **Illustration:** a specific, realistic case.\n4. **Consequence:** what this means for the question.\n\n### Example\n\n*Claim:* Free public transport can reduce traffic in city centres.\n*Mechanism:* Many people drive short distances because the cost of a bus ticket, added up over a month, feels similar to the cost of fuel; removing the fare removes that calculation.\n*Illustration:* A commuter who drives four kilometres to work each day may decide the bus is now the cheaper and less stressful option.\n*Consequence:* If enough commuters make the same decision, congestion falls, which also makes the buses themselves faster and more reliable.\n\n### The test you can apply\n\nRead each body paragraph and ask: *could a reader who disagrees with me follow exactly why I think this?* If the answer depends on the reader already sharing your assumptions, a mechanism link is missing. Adding one or two sentences of mechanism is usually more valuable than adding a third main idea."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (6, 'rich_text', $ipb$Advanced Technique: Concession That Strengthens Your Argument$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"A concession is a point that goes against your position which you acknowledge and then answer. Used well, it shows the examiner that you have considered the issue fully. Used badly, it makes your position unclear.\n\n### The three-move concession\n\n1. **Acknowledge fairly.** State the opposing point in its strongest reasonable form. *It is true that remote work can make it harder for new employees to learn informally from colleagues.*\n2. **Limit it.** Show why it does not defeat your view. *However, this problem affects mainly the first months of employment.*\n3. **Answer it.** Offer a reason or a solution. *Companies can address it by bringing new staff into the office for an initial period while allowing experienced staff more flexibility.*\n\n### Where to place it\n\n- In an agree/disagree essay, the concession can sit at the start of your second body paragraph, or form a short paragraph of its own before the conclusion.\n- In an advantages/disadvantages essay, the concession *is* your weaker side; the answer is your judgement about why it weighs less.\n\n### The danger signal\n\nIf your concession is longer or more convincing than your own argument, your position has drifted. Keep the acknowledgement to one or two sentences and give the answer at least equal space."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (7, 'rich_text', $ipb$Advanced Technique: Topic Sentences That Carry the Argument$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"In a Band 8 essay, you could read only the first sentence of each paragraph and still understand the whole argument. That is a demanding test, and it is one of the fastest ways to improve Coherence and Cohesion.\n\n### Weak topic sentences\n\n- *Firstly, there are many reasons for this.*\n- *Another point is about the environment.*\n- *On the other hand, there are some disadvantages.*\n\nThese sentences announce that a paragraph exists, but say nothing about what it argues.\n\n### Strong topic sentences\n\n- *The most immediate cause is that remote work removed the informal visibility managers once relied on.*\n- *The environmental case is weaker than it first appears, because the savings depend on how electricity is generated.*\n- *The real cost of the policy falls not on drivers but on the businesses that depend on them.*\n\n### Method\n\n1. After planning, write your thesis at the top of your plan.\n2. Under it, write one full topic sentence for each body paragraph *before* you start the introduction.\n3. Check that each topic sentence is a claim that could be disagreed with, not a neutral announcement.\n4. Check that, read together, the topic sentences support the thesis.\n\nThis takes about two minutes and saves time later, because each paragraph now has a clear job."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (8, 'rich_text', $ipb$Advanced Technique: Precise Lexis Instead of Decorative Lexis$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Candidates who want a higher Lexical Resource score often reach for rare words. Examiners, however, are looking for vocabulary that is *precise, natural and appropriate*, not vocabulary that is merely unusual. A rare word used slightly wrongly is worse than a common word used exactly.\n\n### Three precision moves\n\n1. **Replace general verbs with specific ones.** *Affect* and *make* are often too broad. Ask what kind of effect you mean: does the policy *discourage*, *delay*, *undermine*, *encourage* or *accelerate* something?\n2. **Replace general nouns with specific ones.** *Problems* could become *delays*, *shortages*, *costs* or *risks*. *Things* almost always has a better replacement.\n3. **Use collocations, not single words.** Natural writers think in word partnerships: *bear the cost*, *raise awareness*, *a growing body of opinion*, *address the root cause*, *place pressure on*.\n\n### A worked substitution\n\n*Technology has a big effect on young people and causes many problems.*\n\nbecomes\n\n*Constant access to social media can fragment young people's attention and leave less time for sustained reading.*\n\nThe second sentence is not longer for the sake of it; every word now does work. Notice that none of its words are rare.\n\n### What to avoid\n\n- Memorised idioms such as *a double-edged sword* or *every coin has two sides*, which read as rehearsed.\n- Inflated register such as *myriad* or *plethora* when *many* is accurate.\n- Repeating the exact wording of the question; paraphrase the key terms in your introduction and then use natural synonyms throughout."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (9, 'rich_text', $ipb$Advanced Technique: Purposeful Grammatical Range$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Grammatical range is not a checklist of structures to display. At Band 7 and above, complex structures should appear because the meaning needs them. The goal is a mix of sentence types, most of them error-free.\n\n### Structures that naturally serve argument\n\n- **Conditionals to test an idea:** *If councils kept essential services outside the vote, the risk of neglect would fall considerably.*\n- **Relative clauses to add precision:** *Residents who walk the street every day know where the pavement floods.*\n- **Concessive clauses to balance:** *Although the policy is expensive, its long-term savings may justify the cost.*\n- **Noun phrases to compress ideas:** *The removal of fares* instead of *when the fares are removed*.\n- **Passive voice to focus on the action:** *Important infrastructure might be neglected.*\n- **Modal verbs to hedge accurately:** *may*, *could*, *is likely to*, *tends to*.\n\n### Hedging is a grammar skill\n\nStrong essays avoid absolute claims that are easy to disprove. *Technology destroys family life* invites disagreement; *excessive screen use can reduce the time families spend in conversation* is defensible. Hedging with modals and adverbs such as *often*, *largely* and *in many cases* makes your argument more accurate and more mature.\n\n### Accuracy first\n\nIf you are making frequent errors in complex sentences, simplify slightly. A Band 7 profile requires frequent error-free sentences. Two clear sentences are better than one long sentence with three errors."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (10, 'rich_text', $ipb$Worked Example 1: A Band 7-Style Essay$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Prompt (original):** *In some cities, local councils now allow residents to vote directly on how part of the annual budget is spent. Do the advantages of this outweigh the disadvantages?*\n\n**Planning notes (3 minutes):** Question type: advantages versus disadvantages, needs a verdict. Thesis: advantages outweigh, because of local knowledge and trust. Body 1: local knowledge plus sense of responsibility. Body 2: risks of unequal turnout and preference for visible projects. Body 3 (short): how to reduce the risks. Conclusion: verdict with conditions.\n\n---\n\nIn a growing number of cities, councils set aside a share of the annual budget and let residents decide by vote how it should be spent. While this approach has some drawbacks, I believe its benefits are greater, mainly because it builds trust and directs money to problems that officials often overlook.\n\nThe main advantage is that residents usually know their own neighbourhoods better than distant officials do. A council planner may see a street only on a map, whereas the people who walk along it every day know that the lighting is poor or that the pavement floods after rain. When these residents are allowed to vote, small but urgent repairs are more likely to be funded. In addition, taking part in the decision makes people feel responsible for the result, so they may be more willing to accept local taxes in the future.\n\nOn the other hand, there are real risks. Wealthier or better-organised groups tend to vote in larger numbers, which means their projects could win even when other areas need the money more. Moreover, ordinary voters may prefer visible projects, such as a new park, to less exciting but essential ones like replacing old water pipes. If this happens repeatedly, important infrastructure might be neglected.\n\nHowever, these problems can be reduced rather than simply accepted. Councils can keep essential services outside the public vote and allow residents to choose only among additional projects. They can also hold voting in schools, markets and online so that people with less free time still take part.\n\nIn conclusion, direct budget voting is not a perfect system, but as long as it covers only part of the budget and voting is made accessible, its advantages in local knowledge and public trust outweigh its disadvantages.\n\n---\n\n*Word count: approximately 295 words.*"}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (11, 'rich_text', $ipb$Commentary on Worked Example 1$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"This essay shows many typical features of a Band 7 response. It is clearly organised, fully answers the question and is generally accurate. Here is why it is likely to sit at 7 rather than 8.\n\n### Task Response\n\nThe essay addresses both advantages and disadvantages and gives a clear verdict in the introduction and conclusion. The position is consistent throughout. Ideas are extended and supported with plausible examples (poor lighting, flooding pavements, parks versus water pipes). The third body paragraph is a strength: it shows that the writer has thought about how to reduce the risks, which supports the final judgement. To move higher, the main ideas would need slightly fuller development; for example, *more willing to accept local taxes* is asserted rather than explained.\n\n### Coherence and Cohesion\n\nParagraphing is logical and each paragraph has a clear central topic. The progression is easy to follow. However, the linking is somewhat mechanical: *The main advantage is*, *In addition*, *On the other hand*, *Moreover*, *However*, *In conclusion*. These devices are used correctly, but a Band 8 essay would rely more on the ideas themselves and on referencing (*this*, *such projects*) to create flow.\n\n### Lexical Resource\n\nVocabulary is appropriate and generally precise: *set aside a share*, *better-organised groups*, *essential infrastructure*, *neglected*. There is a good range, with some less common items used naturally. Some phrasing is safe rather than precise, such as *real risks* and *less exciting but essential ones*.\n\n### Grammatical Range and Accuracy\n\nThere is a good mix of simple and complex sentences, including relative clauses, conditionals and passive forms, and the vast majority of sentences are error-free. The structures are mostly familiar; a Band 8 essay might show more flexible control, such as fronted clauses or compressed noun phrases, used where the meaning needs them.\n\n### One-line verdict\n\nA clear, well-organised and accurate essay whose main limitation is development depth and slightly formulaic cohesion."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (12, 'rich_text', $ipb$Worked Example 2: A Band 8-Style Essay$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Prompt (original):** *Many companies now use software to monitor how employees spend their working hours. Why is this happening, and is it a positive or negative development?*\n\n**Planning notes (3 minutes):** Question type: two-part direct question. Answer both, in order. Thesis: driven by remote work and demand for measurable output; mostly negative. Body 1: causes (lost visibility, appeal of figures). Body 2: why negative (activity is not value, performative busyness, privacy). Body 3: concession, security monitoring is acceptable, with the distinction stated. Conclusion: restate cause and judgement.\n\n---\n\nSoftware that records how employees spend their working hours, from keystrokes to time spent on particular websites, has become commonplace in many offices. This trend is driven largely by the rise of remote work and by managers' desire for measurable output, yet I would argue that, in most forms, it does more harm than good.\n\nThe spread of such monitoring is easy to explain. Once staff began working from home in large numbers, managers lost the informal visibility they had relied on: they could no longer see who arrived early or who seemed busy. Tracking tools appear to restore that visibility, and they generate neat figures that can be presented to senior leadership as evidence of productivity.\n\nThe difficulty is that these tools measure activity, not value. An analyst who spends an hour thinking a problem through before writing a single line may appear idle, while a colleague who sends dozens of trivial messages looks highly productive. Employees quickly learn to perform busyness for the software, which rewards the wrong behaviour and erodes the trust on which effective teams depend. There are also legitimate privacy concerns, particularly when monitoring extends to personal devices or captures far more data than any manager could reasonably need.\n\nThat said, not all monitoring is objectionable. Logging access to confidential client files, for instance, protects both the company and its customers, and few employees would resent it if its purpose were clearly explained. The real distinction lies between monitoring for security, which is targeted and transparent, and monitoring for control, which is broad and often hidden.\n\nOverall, the growth of workplace tracking reflects understandable anxieties about remote work, but organisations would be better served by judging staff on the quality of their results than on the minutes they log.\n\n---\n\n*Word count: approximately 295 words.*"}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (13, 'rich_text', $ipb$Commentary on Worked Example 2$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"This essay illustrates features typical of Band 8. The difference from Worked Example 1 is not length or difficulty of vocabulary; it is precision and control.\n\n### Task Response\n\nBoth parts of the question are answered fully and in the order asked. The position (*in most forms, it does more harm than good*) is clear and already qualified in the introduction, which prepares the reader for the concession later. Each main idea is developed through a mechanism: managers lost visibility, so tools appear to restore it; tools measure activity, so employees perform activity. The concession paragraph does real argumentative work by drawing a distinction between security and control, which sharpens rather than weakens the position.\n\n### Coherence and Cohesion\n\nThe topic sentences alone tell the story of the argument: the trend is easy to explain; the difficulty is that the tools measure activity, not value; not all monitoring is objectionable. Linking is largely invisible because ideas are sequenced logically. Referencing is skilful: *such monitoring*, *that visibility*, *these tools*, *its purpose*. Notice the near absence of formulaic connectors such as *Firstly* or *Moreover*.\n\n### Lexical Resource\n\nWord choice is precise and natural: *commonplace*, *informal visibility*, *perform busyness*, *erodes the trust*, *legitimate privacy concerns*, *targeted and transparent*. There are skilful contrasts (*activity, not value*; *security* versus *control*) and accurate collocations throughout. Nothing is decorative; each word carries meaning.\n\n### Grammatical Range and Accuracy\n\nSentence structures vary naturally: fronted time clauses (*Once staff began working from home...*), participle and relative clauses, a colon used to introduce explanation, contrastive structures (*while a colleague who sends...*) and a comparative conclusion (*better served by judging... than on...*). Errors are absent or extremely rare, and complexity always serves meaning.\n\n### What to copy, and what not to copy\n\nCopy the method: qualified thesis, mechanism-based paragraphs, a concession that draws a distinction, and topic sentences that carry the argument. Do not copy the sentences. Memorised phrasing transplanted into a different prompt reads as unnatural and can lower your score."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (14, 'rich_text', $ipb$Upgrade Clinic: A Band 6 Paragraph Rewritten$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"### The original paragraph (typical Band 6 features)\n\n*Nowadays technology is very important in education. There are many advantages of using computers in schools. For example students can find information easily and they can learn faster. Also, teachers can use videos and it makes lessons more interesting. So technology is very good for students and schools should use it more.*\n\n### The rewritten paragraph (Band 7–8 features)\n\n*The strongest argument for classroom technology is that it changes how students handle information, not merely how quickly they find it. When a class researches a topic online, pupils must compare sources, judge which are reliable and discard the rest, a skill that a single textbook rarely demands. A history lesson on the causes of a revolution, for instance, becomes an exercise in weighing competing accounts rather than memorising one. Used in this way, technology does not simply make lessons more engaging; it trains the critical judgement that students will need long after they leave school.*\n\n### What changed, and why it matters\n\n1. **One idea instead of three.** The original lists finding information, learning faster and videos. The rewrite develops one idea fully, which serves Task Response far better than a list.\n2. **A claim-led topic sentence.** *Technology is very important* is an announcement; *it changes how students handle information, not merely how quickly they find it* is an arguable claim.\n3. **A mechanism.** The rewrite explains *how* the benefit happens: comparing, judging and discarding sources.\n4. **A specific illustration.** *For example students can find information easily* is generic; the history lesson is concrete and directly supports the mechanism.\n5. **A consequence linked to the wider question.** The final sentence connects the point back to education's purpose.\n6. **Precise vocabulary.** *Very important* and *very good* are replaced by meaning-carrying phrases: *weighing competing accounts*, *critical judgement*.\n7. **Varied, controlled grammar.** A relative clause, a participle opening (*Used in this way*) and a contrastive structure (*does not simply... ; it...*) appear naturally, and the comma error after *For example* is gone."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (15, 'rich_text', $ipb$Error-Diagnosis Table$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Use this table after every practice essay. Find the symptom, confirm the likely cause in your own script, then apply the fix in your next essay. Track which rows appear repeatedly; those are your real bottlenecks.\n\n| Symptom in your essay | Likely cause | Fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| Feedback says \"position unclear\" | Thesis written as a neutral summary, or opinion changes between paragraphs | Build a thesis with the position ladder; check the conclusion repeats the same view |\n| Paragraphs feel thin despite good English | Claim plus example with no mechanism | Add the *how/why* link from the mechanism chain before your example |\n| Essay answers only part of the question | Question type misread during planning | Underline every question word; use the decision-rules table to map one paragraph per part |\n| Linking feels repetitive or forced | Over-reliance on *Moreover*, *Furthermore*, *In addition* | Write claim-led topic sentences; use referencing (*this trend*, *such measures*) |\n| Examples sound invented or exaggerated | Fake statistics or extreme scenarios | Use realistic, everyday cases; never invent figures |\n| Vocabulary errors in \"advanced\" words | Words chosen for rarity, not meaning | Prefer precise common words and checked collocations |\n| Many errors in long sentences | Complexity exceeds control under time pressure | Split into two sentences; keep one complex structure per sentence |\n| Essay under 250 words | Slow planning or over-long introduction | Limit the introduction to two sentences; time each stage |\n| Conclusion introduces a new idea | No plan for the ending | Conclusion = thesis restated plus one-sentence implication |\n| Last paragraph rushed | No time checkpoints | Set checkpoints: plan done by minute 5, body 1 by minute 17, body 2 by minute 30 |"}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (16, 'rich_text', $ipb$Timed Drill 1: Five-Minute Position and Plan$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Time limit: 5 minutes per prompt. Do not write the essay.**\n\nFor each prompt, produce: the question type, a one-sentence thesis built with the position ladder, and two claim-led topic sentences.\n\n1. *Some people believe that universities should require every student to complete a period of work experience before graduating. To what extent do you agree or disagree?*\n2. *In many countries, people are buying fewer physical books and reading more on screens. Is this a positive or negative development?*\n3. *Many cities are introducing charges for drivers who enter the centre at busy times. What are the advantages and disadvantages of this policy?*\n\nCheck your answers against the model plans in **Drill Answers**. Your plans do not need to match; they need to be equally clear and arguable."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (17, 'rich_text', $ipb$Timed Drill 2: Twelve-Minute Body Paragraph$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Time limit: 12 minutes.**\n\nWrite one complete body paragraph of 110–140 words for this prompt:\n\n*Some people say that children today have too much free time spent on screens and that schools should therefore set more homework. Do you agree or disagree?*\n\nYour paragraph must contain all four links of the mechanism chain: claim, mechanism, illustration and consequence. Label each link in the margin after you finish.\n\nThen spend two extra minutes checking: is the topic sentence a claim? Could someone who disagrees follow your reasoning? Is every example realistic?\n\nA model paragraph is in **Drill Answers**."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (18, 'rich_text', $ipb$Timed Drill 3: Eight-Minute Error Hunt$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"**Time limit: 8 minutes.**\n\nThe paragraph below contains eight problems: grammar errors, imprecise vocabulary and one weakness in development. Find and correct them.\n\n*In nowadays, many people works from home and this have a big effect to the society. One advantage is that people save time because they don't need to travel. For example, some people travels two hours every day. Also it is good for the environment. Therefore working from home is very beneficial thing for everybody.*\n\nWrite a corrected version, then compare it with the answer key."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (19, 'rich_text', $ipb$Drill Answers$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"### Drill 1: model plans\n\n**Prompt 1.** Type: agree or disagree. Thesis: *A short, structured work placement would benefit most students because it connects academic study to real practice, although it should be flexible for courses where it adds little value.* Topic sentences: *Work experience gives students a reason to apply theory that lectures alone rarely provide.* / *Making placements compulsory for every course, however, risks becoming a box-ticking exercise unless universities allow exemptions.*\n\n**Prompt 2.** Type: positive or negative. Thesis: *The move to screens is largely positive because it widens access to reading, but it carries a real cost for sustained concentration.* Topic sentences: *Digital reading removes barriers of price and distance that once kept books out of reach.* / *The weakness lies in how screens encourage skimming rather than deep, uninterrupted reading.*\n\n**Prompt 3.** Type: advantages and disadvantages (no verdict required, but a balanced judgement is welcome). Topic sentences: *Charging drivers at peak times changes behaviour more directly than appeals or advertising campaigns.* / *The burden of the charge, however, falls hardest on those with the least flexibility, such as shift workers and small traders.*\n\n### Drill 2: model paragraph\n\n*More homework is unlikely to reduce screen time, because the problem is not how much free time children have but what makes screens so attractive within it. Most entertainment apps are designed to reward short bursts of attention, so a child who finishes an extra worksheet will often return to the screen immediately afterwards. A pupil given an additional hour of maths, for example, may simply move her gaming to later in the evening and sleep less as a result. Extra homework therefore risks adding pressure without changing the habit it is meant to address.*\n\nClaim: first sentence. Mechanism: apps reward short bursts, so the habit returns. Illustration: the pupil moving her gaming later. Consequence: more pressure, unchanged habit.\n\n### Drill 3: answer key\n\n1. *In nowadays* → *Nowadays*. 2. *people works* → *people work*. 3. *this have* → *this has*. 4. *a big effect to the society* → *a considerable effect on society*. 5. *some people travels* → *some people travel*. 6. *Also it is good for the environment* is undeveloped: add a mechanism, e.g. *fewer daily journeys mean less fuel burned*. 7. *very beneficial thing* → *very beneficial* (or *a very beneficial thing*). 8. *for everybody* is an overgeneralisation → *for many employees*.\n\nCorrected version: *Nowadays, many people work from home, and this has had a considerable effect on society. One clear advantage is the time saved on commuting; some employees previously spent two hours travelling every day. There is also an environmental benefit, since fewer daily journeys mean less fuel burned. Working from home is therefore highly beneficial for many employees.*"}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (20, 'callout', $ipb$When You Are Running Out of Time$ipb$, $ipb${"label":"Emergency rule","body":"If you reach minute 33 and have not started your second body paragraph, stop polishing. Write a three-sentence paragraph (claim, mechanism, short example), then a two-sentence conclusion that restates your thesis. A complete essay with a short final paragraph protects Task Response far better than an excellent essay with no ending. Use the last two minutes to check verb agreement and articles, the errors most often missed under pressure."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (21, 'rich_text', $ipb$14-Day Advanced Repair Schedule$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"This schedule assumes about 60–75 minutes a day. Write by hand or type, matching the format you will use on test day.\n\n| Day | Focus | Task |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Diagnosis | Write one full essay in 40 minutes. Mark it against the error-diagnosis table; note your top three rows. |\n| 2 | Question types | Plan six prompts using the decision-rules table (5 minutes each). No full essays. |\n| 3 | Position ladder | Build theses for five prompts; rewrite the thesis of your Day 1 essay. |\n| 4 | Mechanism chain | Write three body paragraphs (12 minutes each), labelling every link. |\n| 5 | Full essay | 40-minute essay applying Days 2–4. Compare with Day 1. |\n| 6 | Concession | Add a three-move concession to two previous essays; write one new concession paragraph. |\n| 7 | Review | Reread all work so far. Rewrite your weakest paragraph from scratch. |\n| 8 | Topic sentences | Plan four essays by topic sentences only; check they tell the whole argument. |\n| 9 | Lexis | Highlight every general word (*thing*, *problem*, *affect*, *good*) in two essays and replace each precisely. |\n| 10 | Grammar | Find every sentence with an error; rewrite each, splitting over-long sentences. |\n| 11 | Full essay | 40-minute essay on a two-part question. Time each checkpoint. |\n| 12 | Upgrade clinic | Take three Band 6-style paragraphs from your own work and rewrite them using the clinic method. |\n| 13 | Full essay | 40-minute essay under strict conditions, then a 15-minute self-score. |\n| 14 | Consolidation | Compare Day 1, 5, 11 and 13 essays. List what changed and the one habit still to fix. |\n\nIf possible, get feedback from a qualified teacher on the Day 5 and Day 13 essays. Self-assessment is valuable, but an outside reader catches problems you cannot see in your own writing."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (22, 'rich_text', $ipb$Self-Scoring Protocol Before Feedback$ipb$, $ipb${"markdown":"Before you show an essay to anyone, score it yourself with these questions. Answering them honestly makes outside feedback far more useful, because you will notice where your judgement and the reader's differ.\n\n### Task Response questions\n\n- Did I answer every part of the question, in the order asked?\n- Can I point to my position in the introduction, the body and the conclusion?\n- Does each body paragraph contain a mechanism, not only a claim and an example?\n\n### Coherence and Cohesion questions\n\n- Do my topic sentences alone tell the argument?\n- How many formulaic connectors did I use? Could referencing replace some of them?\n- Does each paragraph have exactly one central idea?\n\n### Lexical Resource questions\n\n- Which three words in this essay are the most precise? Which three are the vaguest?\n- Did I use any word I am not certain about? If so, what did I mean by it?\n- Did I repeat the question's wording, or paraphrase it?\n\n### Grammatical Range and Accuracy questions\n\n- How many sentences contain an error? Which error types repeat?\n- Did I use complex structures because the meaning needed them?\n- Are my claims hedged where they should be?\n\nWrite your answers in two or three lines under the essay. Over two weeks, these notes become a personal record of what still holds your score down."}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (23, 'faq', $ipb$Advanced Lab FAQ$ipb$, $ipb${"items":[{"question":"Should I write more than 300 words to look more advanced?","answer":"Not necessarily. The minimum is 250 words, and most strong essays fall between about 270 and 320. Longer essays take more time to check and often contain more errors. Aim for full development within a length you can control in 40 minutes."},{"question":"Is it risky to include a counter-argument?","answer":"Only if you leave it unanswered or make it stronger than your own case. A brief, fair concession followed by a clear response usually strengthens your position."},{"question":"Can I use the same structure for every essay?","answer":"A core method is useful, but the structure should change with the question type. A two-part question needs a paragraph for each part; an advantages-versus-disadvantages question needs a verdict. Rigid structures are a common reason for partial answers."},{"question":"How do I know if my vocabulary is precise enough?","answer":"Ask whether a reader could misunderstand the word or replace it with something more exact. If a sentence contains general words such as thing, problem or affect, there is usually a more precise option."},{"question":"Should I write Task 2 before Task 1?","answer":"Many candidates do, because Task 2 counts more. If you choose this order, practise it beforehand and set a firm stop time so Task 1 still receives about 20 minutes."},{"question":"How many practice essays do I need before test day?","answer":"There is no fixed number. Quality of correction matters more than quantity: one essay that you diagnose, rewrite and learn from is worth more than several essays written and forgotten."}]}$ipb$::jsonb),
    (24, 'checklist', $ipb$Advanced Lab Final Checklist$ipb$, $ipb${"items":["I identify the question type and map one paragraph to each part before writing.","My thesis is built with the position ladder: opinion, condition, reason and limit.","Each body paragraph opens with a claim-led topic sentence.","Every main idea contains claim, mechanism, illustration and consequence.","Any concession is acknowledged briefly, limited and answered.","My examples are realistic and contain no invented statistics.","I choose precise, natural words and collocations rather than rare or memorised phrases.","Complex structures appear because the meaning needs them, and most sentences are error-free.","My conclusion restates the position without adding new ideas.","I reach my time checkpoints and keep two minutes to check agreement, articles and spelling.","After each essay, I record my top recurring rows from the error-diagnosis table."]}$ipb$::jsonb)
)
